By contrast, option A satisfies every stated boundary with the smallest change: the core universal–cell-label–UMI–poly(dT) architecture on the existing magnetic beads stays identical, while a modest fraction of additional TCR-specific capture oligos—tethered to the same bead surface and positioned immediately upstream of the V(D)J junction—directly enriches the CDR3-containing segment at the initial hybridization step already performed inside the cartridge. Reverse transcription therefore begins only a few dozen nucleotides before the junction, placing CDR3 reliably inside the unchanged 225 bp Read 2 window without altering RT chemistry, UMI linkage, single-cell barcoding throughput, or panel compatibility. The geometry objection is thereby solved at the capture stage itself, and no wholesale bead resynthesis or new template-switching chemistry is required.

Options C and D still begin from the distant 3′ end or presuppose an upstream fragment never captured; E violates the explicit cost constraint. Supplementing the validated beads with targeted TCR oligos therefore recovers CDR3 while preserving every element of the already-optimized workflow exactly as the student requested.}

